Below is a minimal snippet you can drop into your Node‑JS script (or run from a REPL) that calls the **classfloadhk005\_runIt** helper.  
The helper will load the target class from `/path/to/classes`, invoke its “instrumentation” routine, and then compare the old (`12345L`) vs. new (`54321L`) values while writing all diagnostic messages to the supplied `PrintStream`.

> ⚠️ **Important:**  
> * Make sure the `logStream` instance actually implements Java’s `java.io.PrintStream`.  
> * If you’re running inside a pure Java environment instead of Node.js, replace the `await` / Promise logic with the appropriate synchronous invocation pattern.

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Import the tooling interface – usually exposed automatically.
//     If it isn’t already available globally, import it explicitly:
// const { functions } = require('your-toolkit-path');

// 2️⃣  Prepare the arguments exactly as required by the wrapper:
//
//      • argv   → A plain JavaScript array containing the CLI args.
//      • out    → Reference name or serialized form of the PrintStream.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
(async () => {
  try {
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Call the wrapped Java test harness:
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const testResult = await functions.classfloadhk005_runIt({
      argv: ['/path/to/classes', '60'],   // <-- path + timeout/value flag
      out : 'logStream'                   // <- Name/reference of PrintStream
    });

    console.log('--- Test finished ---');
    console.dir(testResult);              // Inspect whatever the hook returns
  }
  catch(err){
    console.error('Error executing runIt:', err);
  }
})();
```

### What happens under the hood?

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| 1 | Loads the compiled classes located at `/path/to/classes`. |
| 2 | Calls the static entry point defined by the test harness (usually something akin to `<ClassName>.main(String[])`). |
| 3 | Inside that main, the harness performs the following checks:<br>- Reads the field holding the original long constant (`12345L`).<br>- Applies the bytecode transformation/instrumentation.<br>- Verifies that the updated constant equals `54321L`. |
| 4 | All logs (e.g., “original=…”, “transformed=…”) are streamed through the passed `PrintStream` named `logStream`. |

If everything goes right, the returned `testResult` will indicate success (often `{success:true}`), otherwise it’ll contain error details so you can debug why the instrumentation failed.

Feel free to tweak the argument list or add more diagnostics depending on how your specific testing framework reports failures. Happy debugging!

